@@ -59,6 +59,16 @@ With `DM_SCREEN_ASPECT=1.43` (launcher setting *Picture shape*, offered because
 
 Checked on a Mac (M1, macOS 27, headless, an island save) against 16:9: the 3D view keeps 16:9's sides and shows more above and below; the title's version, press-A and settings lines, the island's ZL icon, clock, minimap and button guides sit at the new edges; the pockets, the phone (its backdrop's light band stretched) and the save dialog keep their shape. On an iPhone Duo (iOS 27.1) an earlier build of this looked right to its player. Open: an NPC's dialog box stays at its 16:9 place (-320), not at the taller screen's bottom edge; the phone's blurred backdrop shows a hard edge at its top left.
 
+## iPhone portrait (current)
+
+A phone standing up shows the game's 16:9 picture above the touch controls, not a portrait picture: `dm/ios/Sources/DmLayout.h` gives the picture the height its own width asks for, puts it at the safe area's top and gives the rest of the window to the band under it, where `DmGamepad.m`'s portrait anchors draw the pad (the two thumb groups at 0.28 and 0.72 of the width, 0.58 down) over a panel view the front end keeps for what the game's own screens cannot show there. A 390x844 phone (iPhone 14 and the like) gets a 390x219 picture with no bars above or below it and a 390x566 band; the safe areas are kept whole, so the notch and the home indicator are clear of both. Halving that window instead left the picture 219 of the upper part's 422 points, bars making up the other 48 % of it. The picture never takes more than half the window: an iPad's 4:3 asks for more than half, so it keeps the midpoint split it had, and an iPad mini's 1.52 picture is its own height either way, with only the bars it used to have going to the band. A fold sets both edges itself.
+
+The picture keeps 16:9 because a phone's long side is 1.78 to 2.16 times its short one, and 16:9 across the full width is what leaves the band the room a phone standing up has. The front end therefore resolves *Picture shape* `auto` to `0` (16:9) for a window taller than it is wide whose long side is 1.77 or more, and keeps the window's own shape for an iPad or an unfolded Duo (1.33 to 1.52), where the picture is taller than 16:9.
+
+**`auto` is resolved once, at the game's start**, because `DM_SCREEN_ASPECT` is written into the frame step state page and the game builds its window textures from it. A game started in portrait keeps its 16:9 picture after the phone turns to landscape (with black bars at the sides); to get the wider picture of [Screen shape](#screen-shape-dm_screen_aspect-current) on a 19.5:9 phone, start in landscape. Turning a landscape game to portrait keeps the wide picture, fitted into the upper part.
+
+**A phone standing up is handheld, so the game's own keyboard takes touches.** The profile sets no `op_mode`, so the game starts docked and the host hands it no touch points (`touch_read` in `hle_hid.c`), which leaves the game's keyboard unreachable. The front end therefore switches the console mode by the screen's orientation while no TV shows the picture (`followOrientation` in `front_ios.m`): standing up it goes handheld and lying down it returns to the mode the player had, a choice made in the in-game menu included. The game reads touches in both modes, so nothing else has to change, and the switch is the console mode change notification the game watches, so it follows while it runs. Handheld also renders at 1280x720, so the picture is the handheld one while the phone stands up. *Console mode* in the in-game menu (or `DM_OPMODE=0` in `Documents/env.txt`) still works for a phone lying down.
+
 ## Mac routes
 
 - From a fresh save, `DM_KEYS="40:A*51/2,145:B*12/0.4,151:Down,152:Right*4/0.4,154.5:A,155.5:Right*4/0.4,158:A,159:Down*2/0.4,160.5:Left*2/0.4,162:A,163.5:Plus,167:A*4/2,175:Plus,178:A*146/2,179:Plus*30/4"` names the player "tom" on the keyboard, takes the defaults, watches the movie and reaches the dock at about frame 14,100 (470 s).
